* @title Gimpel BNORM, INORM, IMAGE, LINE, and HYPHENATE paragraph-formatting helpers
* @attribution James F. Gimpel, Algorithms in SNOBOL4, Chapter 10 (BNORM 10.1, INORM 10.2, IMAGE 10.4, LINE 10.3, HYPHENATE 10.7); Catspaw SNOBOL4+ program diskette
* @options {"case": false}
* @expect >>>
* BNORM('ABCD<BS><BS><BS><BS>1234'): A+1B+2C+3D+4
* INORM('B<BS>A'): A+B
* IMAGE pass 1: 'AB'
* IMAGE pass 2: 'CD'
* LINE CW=10:
*   'A QUICK'
*   'BROWN FOX'
*   'JUMPED'
*   'OVER THE'
*   'LAZY DOG.'
* HYPHENATE('niatbo',3) = 4 HYPHEN=[-]
* HYPHENATE('ssensselerac',6) = 8 HYPHEN=[-]
* HYPHENATE('gninniw',3) = 4 HYPHEN=[-]
* HYPHENATE('sessenrah',3) = 6 HYPHEN=[-]
* <<<
*
* Chapter 10's paragraph-formatting routines all collaborate around two
* control characters: BSPACE (ASCII 0x08, used to encode overstrikes)
* and USCORE ('_'). The book's published programs treat both as
* externally-supplied globals; the Catspaw distribution initialises
* them in each .INC via the SNOBOL4+ CHAR(8) extension. Snoflake (v3.11
* IBM SNOBOL4 macros) does not implement CHAR, so the includes are
* patched to derive BSPACE the standard SNOBOL4 way:
*     &ALPHABET TAB(8) LEN(1) . BSPACE
* See SPACING.INC for the early-bind shim that ensures these globals
* exist before any pattern using them is constructed.
*
* HYPHENATE consumes the reversed form of a word (e.g. 'niatbo' for
* 'obtain') and walks lowercase character classes that don't match
* under the default case-fold mode. The fixture therefore runs with
* `case: false` so SPAN(LOWERS_ '-') sees its argument unmodified.
* All other identifiers and data are uppercase, so preserve-case has
* no observable effect on BNORM / INORM / IMAGE / LINE.
-INCLUDE "LINE.INC"
-INCLUDE "BNORM.INC"
-INCLUDE "INORM.INC"
-INCLUDE "IMAGE.INC"
-INCLUDE "HYPHENAT.INC"
	BS = BSPACE

* BNORM (Program 10.1, p. 191). 'ABCD' followed by four BSPACEs and
* then '1234' overprints each letter of 'ABCD' with the digit. After
* B-normalization the string has each print position grouped together
* ('A+1B+2C+3D+4', with + standing in for BSPACE for visibility).
	OUTPUT = "BNORM('ABCD<BS><BS><BS><BS>1234'): "
+	REPLACE(BNORM('ABCD' BS BS BS BS '1234'), BS, '+')

* INORM (Program 10.2, p. 199). Overstruck characters that produce the
* same image are sorted into a canonical (alphabetic) order so that
* 'B<BS>A' and 'A<BS>B' both normalise to 'A<BS>B'.
	OUTPUT = "INORM('B<BS>A'): " REPLACE(INORM('B' BS 'A'), BS, '+')

* IMAGE (Program 10.4, p. 198). Given a B-normalized string, return
* the I-th print pass. 'AB' followed by two backspaces and 'CD'
* overprints 'AB' with 'CD'; B-normalization arranges the characters
* as 'A<BS>C B<BS>D' so pass 1 is 'AB' and pass 2 is 'CD'.
	S = BNORM('AB' BS BS 'CD')
	OUTPUT = "IMAGE pass 1: '" IMAGE(S, 1) "'"
	OUTPUT = "IMAGE pass 2: '" IMAGE(S, 2) "'"

* LINE (Program 10.3, p. 202). The book's worked example: fill the
* sentence into 10-character lines with justification and hyphenation
* off. HYPHENATE/JUSTIFY are LINE.INC globals, set to 1 by the module
* init; the test overrides them to match the book's first listing.
	LINE_INIT('A QUICK BROWN FOX JUMPED OVER THE LAZY DOG.')
	JUSTIFY = 0
	HYPH_FLAG = HYPHENATE
	HYPHENATE = 0
	OUTPUT = "LINE CW=10:"
L1	OUTPUT = "  '" LINE(10) "'"     :S(L1)

* HYPHENATE (Program 10.7, p. 205). The book's worked example
* states "HYPHENATE('niatbo', 3) will just succeed and return a
* value of 4" with HYPHEN set to '-'. The reversed-word inputs
* below correspond to 'obtain' (ob-tain), 'carelessness'
* (care-lessness), 'winning' (win-ning), and 'harnesses'
* (har-nesses) per the suffix-stripping discussion on pp. 206-207.
* HYPHENATE was shadowed by a JUSTIFY flag above; restore it.
	HYPHENATE = HYPH_FLAG
	K = HYPHENATE('niatbo', 3)
	OUTPUT = "HYPHENATE('niatbo',3) = " K ' HYPHEN=[' HYPHEN ']'
	K = HYPHENATE('ssensselerac', 6)
	OUTPUT = "HYPHENATE('ssensselerac',6) = " K ' HYPHEN=[' HYPHEN ']'
	K = HYPHENATE('gninniw', 3)
	OUTPUT = "HYPHENATE('gninniw',3) = " K ' HYPHEN=[' HYPHEN ']'
	K = HYPHENATE('sessenrah', 3)
	OUTPUT = "HYPHENATE('sessenrah',3) = " K ' HYPHEN=[' HYPHEN ']'

END
